so welcome to  this first time inaugural digital  medicine symposium here at Yale my name  is Greg Nicolai I teach at the School of  Management as adjunct faculty I'm also a  chief medical officer and chief medical  information officer for a large public  company and I've worked in health care  throughout my life and also a graduate  of the Yale School of Medicine today we  today we wanted to bring a discussion of  digital medicine to Yale in in order to  really stimulate some thought and really  begin to lay the foundation and explore  what is digital medicine but but perhaps  more importantly why digital medicine  why now and why should we be  enthusiastic about it and to be to be  honest today started out as a little bit  of a pilot a little bit of an experiment  really testing the waters to see what  was the level of interest in in the Yale  community and we had an overwhelmingly  positive response from both from from  the audience well we'll be expecting  people to be kind of wandering in  throughout the day as well as from  sponsors and you know willing to help  fund this and and our collaborators  who've really done a terrific job  and and again that kind of brings us  back to the question why why the  overwhelming response and and why why  the interest at this point and I guess  the other you know key key thing to to  mention is that this is the first Yale  digital medicine symposium in about a  month  there is a Harvard digital medicine  symposium so we're very happy to I take  the lead and allow our colleagues and  Cambridge to be fast followers so so why  did little medicine and and really in a  phrase digital medicine is about patient  empowerment  empowering patients with more choices  more information and really bringing  bringing the the health care which was  previously in the hands of a small group  of incumbents doctors insurance  providers etc into the hands of patients  why do you medicine where where does  some of these promises come from today  we'll hear examples on how digital  medicine is improving all these things I  mean really it's a it's a stunning  series of possibilities you'll hear  about talk space is improving access as  well as reducing stigma in behavioral  health and depression and and other  other types of mental disorders you'll  hear about AI is improving the use of  personalization as well as building a  stronger evidence base for for medicine  we'll talk about the use of data and out  to generate better outcomes which will  also create but better evidence for a  wider more representative population  we'll hear about how the collaboration  between Apple and Johnson &amp; Johnson -  the most recognized names in healthcare  or just who the most recognized brand  names have come together to create a  sweeping offering for patients you know  - for more convenience about about  healthcare and and the the evidence is  there perhaps this is one of the most  welcomed surprises that digital medicine  actually produces clinical results and  you know just to mention some of the  examples diabetes measurable  improvements in diabetes care measure  measurable improvements  in asthma treatments and respiratory  disease measurable survival in lung  cancer for patients who use digital  disease management apps but in order to  get there we have to proceed responsibly  so part of the mission here is not just  to talk to to this audience about what  the promises are but really to send out  a sense of urgency that that in order  for this new feel to progress standards  need to be met and those standards are  not to encumber progress but actually to  help deliver progress to create a  foundation of better sense of security  and a better sense of confidence that  safety exists quality exists we need an  interdisciplinary approach between  medicine business technology educate the  stakeholders and we need innovative  business models to be able to pay for  this and to achieve the promises that  that we want to we want to get so today  so today's audience or today's target  audience if you will is the the non  expert in digital medicine but people  who are informed and care deeply about  health care and want to learn and think  about the promises the that what did the  definitions we'll go over briefly what's  the status of the progress in the field  of digital medicine and implicit here  will be some of the promises that that  we mentioned suffice to say that digital  healthcare digital medicine is a  complicated landscape everything from  mobile apps personalized healthcare  telemedicine etc will be focused more on  digital therapeutics and some of the  other enablers you know we'll be talking  about drug supply chain  as as an enabler but this entire field  is moving forward with really an  impressive speed again why digital  medicine digital medicine has clinical  impact Digital saw a software healthcare  software has been really putting up some  impressive results we'll we'll hear a  little bit later on about behavioral  health software that is that is creating  you know promises for in treatment of  ADHD as well as other other types of  behavioral disorders as mentioned  diabetes etc lung cancer survival why  why is this happening now part of it is  because there is there have been some  landmark approvals coming from the FDA  and and even in drug adherence which you  know my colleagues in medicine that know  that this has been a very difficult area  to to get our heads around and to create  benefits even in drug adherence using  digital technology and a whole  wraparound system have demonstrated a  benefit of improving adherence in  patients who are defined as medically  refractory to treatment not not  psychologically or and not you know  disorganized but medically refractory  patients had clinical benefits once they  improved their adherence and you know  and and the opportunity for public  health management in monitoring disease  and air quality for asthma and it's not  stopping with this there's a host of  other programs that are moving forward  in digital medicine sleep cognitive  training etc etc digital medicine also  has regulatory acceptance so the FDA in  recent years has really been very  forward-thinking there  progressive about assuring this new era  starting with the 20th century 21st  century cures act I'll go through these  as a number of quick examples because  there's really just too many to belabor  the 21st century cures at led to the  digital health innovation Action Plan in  2017 a pre certification program which  keeps on being updated most recently  this year as well as a new approach  certifying artificial intelligence  products  and as well as a commitment to  real-world evidence which is is has to  be enabled using different digital  databases and digital techniques and  finally digital medicine is well funded  part of the reason that that us you know  all of us who have been observers of  this digital transformation have been  waiting for this to happen well you know  follow the money so its projected to be  over a two hundred billion dollar market  in the next decade venture funding is up  the consumer market for for data which  we'll hear about shortly is it is  growing very rapidly as well as  artificial intelligence and if you just  look at the numbers you know they speak  for themselves a thirty percent  year-over-year increase in venture  funding increasing deal sizes an  increasing number of deals in in digital  healthcare so so why digital medicine  digital medicine as we said is about  patient empowerment but I actually  struggled with with what this actually  means because it's really not about the  digital it's not even about the medicine  and it's not even in my opinion about  the patient it's about the empowerment  and what I mean is that in the near  future we're not going to be talking  about digital medicine we will be  talking about this as the standard of  care as a standard of practice and  ultimately it's because organizations  that are interested in healthcare are  shifting their focus to  what is engaging patients when you hear  the CEO of CVS and Aetna talk about what  was what was the main driver of a  seventy billion dollar merger their  answer was patient engagement when when  you look at what is funding artificial  intelligence and why insurance companies  are pouring billions of dollars their  answer is patient engagement because  we're at the era where we're seeing  power shifting from the incumbents from  physicians from payers from health  systems where we used to you own the  information we used to own the decision  and we forced it on in almost a  belittling way calling you patients who  could not be involved in your decision  of of healthcare the shift is  empowerment of all of us so that we all  can engage and participate in healthcare  to actually become the people providing  our own healthcare that's why I think we  should all be hopeful and very  cautiously optimistic about about that  future so that being said let's move on  to the discussion we will try to save  time for a couple of questions at the  end of each end of each talk will will  talk about data and outcomes we will  talk about use of artificial  intelligence drug supply therapeutics  and we we have to give time to think  about the ethical considerations and the  responsibility that we all have and  before I'll ask Harlan to come up I just  want to say thank you to our organizers  who've done a terrific job particularly  cbut and and you know here we are in  this gorgeous building at the at the  Yale Law School as well as our sponsors  including a Medtronic Stewart health  health Haven hub  health care ventures as well as a PRA  health and a little bit of a plug for a  future pitch competition coming up  sponsored by health haven hub and health  ventures which is our homegrown digital  medicine incubator and venture fund
 

